{
  "assessment": "The source packet provides a complete experimental and theoretical study of the spiral Ising antiferromagnet Nd3BWO9, focusing on the critical endpoint (CEP) at mu0Hc ~ 1.04 T and Tc ~ 0.3 K, the supercritical scaling h ~ t^(beta+gamma) with beta+gamma ~ 1.563, the universal Gruneisen ratio scaling Gamma_H ~ 1/t^(beta+gamma-1), and the magnetocaloric cooling to 195 mK. The extraction focuses on the supercritical scaling of specific heat peak positions and the Gruneisen ratio peak/dip values, which form a coherent quantitative cluster with two regimes (above and below Tc) and two readouts. The source provides explicit equations, parameter values, and experimental conditions, enabling a deterministic Oracle response. The case is authoring-ready.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Xinyang Liu, Ising Supercriticality and Universal Magnetocalorics in Spiral Antiferromagnet Nd3BWO9, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/nqcw-pz8v",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/nqcw-pz8v",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2601.07810v4"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper reports thermodynamic and magnetocaloric measurements on the spiral Ising antiferromagnet Nd3BWO9, identifying a critical endpoint (CEP) at mu0Hc ~ 1.04 T and Tc ~ 0.3 K, and an Ising supercritical regime above it. It establishes universal scaling laws for specific heat, magnetic susceptibility, and the magnetic Gruneisen ratio, and demonstrates adiabatic demagnetization cooling to 195 mK. The extraction focuses on the supercritical scaling of specific heat peak positions and the Gruneisen ratio peak/dip values, which form a coherent quantitative cluster with two regimes (above and below Tc) and two readouts.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "supercritical_scaling",
      "statement": "Near the critical endpoint, the singular part of the free energy has a universal form F = t^(2-alpha) * phi_F(h * t^(-beta-gamma)), leading to universal scaling of thermodynamic quantities. The specific heat peak positions follow h ~ t^(beta+gamma), and the magnetic Gruneisen ratio peak/dip values follow Gamma_H ~ t^(1-beta-gamma).",
